# .gitignore — allowlist ("whitelist") style.
#
# Everything is ignored by default; only the paths allowed below are tracked. A new file therefore
# has to be admitted on purpose, so build output, editor scratch, downloaded dependencies and
# machine-local state stay out of the repository by construction instead of by remembering a rule for
# every new kind of artifact. It is what keeps the repository at ~15 MB while a working tree with
# caches, `zig-out/`, screenshots and the retired WebView2 payload is ~670 MB.
#
# To start tracking something new, add a `!` line for it — and for the directories on the way to it.
#
# Deliberately untracked, and kept on the `reference` branch instead — everything that is not part
# of the program itself: `ARCHITECTURE.md` and `design.md` (the engineering rules, the decisions and
# the original product brief), `archive/` (the retired WebView2 shell and its Node suites), `docs/`
# (the interface specifications), `dev/` (the build driver, the GUI harness and its baselines, the
# path audit) and `tools/` (the dependency fetcher, the end-to-end suites, and the process-layer
# experiments, which even carried two compiled `.exe` files). `master` carries only what a build reads
# and what the program offers: `.gitignore`, `build.zig`, `src/`, `third_party/` and `assets/`. The
# branch also carries the comments that cite those documents; `master` is self-contained without them.

# ── 1. Deny everything… ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*
# …except directories, so the rules below can reach inside them. A file inside an allowed directory
# is still ignored unless a later rule allows it explicitly.
!*/

# ── 2. Repository metadata and the build entry point ─────────────────────────
!.gitignore
!build.zig

# ── 3. What the program is: sources and the data it offers ───────────────────
!src/**
!assets/**

# ── 4. Vendored GUI dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only what the build reads: the ImGui sources, the SDL3 headers/libs (including the DLL that ships
# next to the executable), and the WebView2 shim header the picker's GUID stand-in compiles against.
# The rest of third_party/webview2 is the retired WebView2 shell's payload (rule 9) — 45 MB nothing
# builds from any more — plus the downloaded .nupkg; `third_party/_dl/` holds fetch output.
!third_party/NATIVE-GUI-NOTICE.md
!third_party/imgui/**
!third_party/sdl3/**
!third_party/webview2/shim_include/**
!third_party/webview2/LICENSE.txt
!third_party/webview2/NOTICE.txt

# ── 5. …and inside the allowed trees, keep generated and scratch content out again ──
# (Ignoring a directory excludes everything under it, so these cannot be re-admitted by `dev/**`.)
/zig-out/
/.zig-cache/
/.zig-global-cache/
/crashdumps/
/archive/
/dev/_shot/
/dev/_native-tmp/
/dev/_build/
/.pipelith/
/.pipelith-test/
/.pipelith-test-empty/
/third_party/_dl/

# ── 6. Compiled artifacts are never tracked, even inside an allowed tree ─────
# (Their sources are what belongs here. `SDL3.dll` / `libSDL3.dll.a` are the vendored SDL3 build
# inputs, so they are matched by neither rule.)
*.exe
*.pdb
*.obj
*.o
